extra_css='''.beginner{background:#fffdf4;border-left:5px solid #b7791f;padding:14px 16px;border-radius:9px;margin:14px 0}.terms{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(240px,1fr));gap:9px;margin:10px 0}.term{background:#f8fbfd;border:1px solid #dce6ee;border-radius:8px;padding:10px}.term b{color:#173f5f}.mental{background:#f4f8ff;border:1px solid #cbdcf0;border-radius:9px;padding:13px;margin:12px 0}.walk{background:#f8fff8;border:1px solid #d1e7d1;border-radius:9px;padding:13px}.why{background:#f7f7fb;border-left:4px solid #6b46c1;padding:11px 13px;border-radius:7px}.plain{font-size:1.03em}'''
s=s.replace('</style></head>',extra_css+'</style><meta name="beginner-foundation-count" content="21"></head>',1)

primer='''<section class="card" id="networking-foundations"><h2>Networking foundations: read this first if these topics are new</h2><p class="plain">A network device has a <b>control plane</b>, which learns topology and routes and decides what should happen, and a <b>data plane</b>, which forwards real packets according to those decisions. A protocol can look healthy in the control plane while a later forwarding dependency is broken.</p><div class="terms"><div class="term"><b>Route</b><br>A rule saying how to reach a destination prefix.</div><div class="term"><b>Prefix</b><br>An IP network such as 10.20.0.0/16. Longer masks describe more specific destinations.</div><div class="term"><b>Next hop</b><br>The next router or tunnel endpoint toward the destination.</div><div class="term"><b>RIB — Routing Information Base</b><br>The software routing table where candidate and active routes are evaluated.</div><div class="term"><b>FIB — Forwarding Information Base</b><br>The optimized forwarding table programmed into hardware or the operating-system kernel.</div><div class="term"><b>Recursive next-hop resolution</b><br>If a route points to 192.0.2.1, the router must also know how to reach 192.0.2.1, repeating lookups until a directly usable adjacency is found.</div><div class="term"><b>Adjacency</b><br>The concrete outgoing interface and Layer-2 neighbor information needed to send the packet.</div><div class="term"><b>Underlay</b><br>The ordinary IP network that transports tunnel packets.</div><div class="term"><b>Overlay</b><br>A logical network built on the underlay. VXLAN is an overlay.</div><div class="term"><b>VTEP</b><br>VXLAN Tunnel Endpoint: the device that adds/removes VXLAN headers.</div><div class="term"><b>VNI</b><br>VXLAN Network Identifier: the 24-bit logical segment identifier inside VXLAN.</div><div class="term"><b>EVPN</b><br>Ethernet VPN: a BGP-based control plane that advertises MACs, IPs, Ethernet-segment information and IP prefixes for overlays.</div><div class="term"><b>Route Target (RT)</b><br>Metadata that decides which VPN/EVPN routes a tenant imports or exports.</div><div class="term"><b>Route Distinguisher (RD)</b><br>A value that makes overlapping VPN routes unique inside BGP. RD is uniqueness; RT is policy.</div><div class="term"><b>IRB</b><br>Integrated Routing and Bridging: one device bridges same-subnet traffic and routes between subnets.</div><div class="term"><b>Anycast gateway</b><br>Multiple leaves present the same virtual default-gateway IP/MAC so hosts retain one gateway identity during mobility.</div><div class="term"><b>BUM</b><br>Broadcast, Unknown-unicast and Multicast traffic, which needs flooding/replication behavior.</div><div class="term"><b>Redistribution</b><br>Taking a route from one protocol and originating a new version into another protocol.</div><div class="term"><b>Administrative Distance / Route Preference</b><br>A local ranking used when different protocols offer the same destination. Vendor defaults differ.</div><div class="term"><b>LACP</b><br>Link Aggregation Control Protocol, used to make multiple Ethernet links operate as one logical bundle.</div><div class="term"><b>MLAG / vPC / VSX / MC-LAG</b><br>Vendor-specific ways for two switches to coordinate a multi-chassis Ethernet bundle.</div><div class="term"><b>EVPN multihoming</b><br>A standards-based way to connect one Ethernet segment to multiple EVPN provider edges using an ESI, DF behavior, aliasing and split horizon.</div></div><div class="mental"><b>The five-question troubleshooting model:</b><ol><li>Did the protocol learn the route or endpoint?</li><li>Did policy permit it and did the RIB select/install it?</li><li>Can the next hop be recursively resolved?</li><li>Did the device program the FIB/adjacency/tunnel state?</li><li>Does the actual packet path, MTU, VNI/VLAN, neighbor state and return path work?</li></ol></div><div class="walk"><b>Example:</b> If a leaf learns EVPN Type-5 prefix 10.50.0.0/24 with next hop 192.0.2.50, that proves only that BGP learned it. The leaf must import it into the correct tenant VRF, select it in the RIB, resolve 192.0.2.50 through the underlay, program a FIB entry, encapsulate the packet toward the VTEP, and the remote leaf must deliver it. The return path must also work.</div></section>'''
# Place primer immediately after executive dashboard.
m=re.search(r'(<section class="card"><h2>Executive dashboard</h2>.*?</section>)',s,re.S)
if not m: raise SystemExit('Executive dashboard not found')
s=s[:m.end()]+primer+s[m.end():]

F=[
('Routed overlay, EVPN, Ethernet Segment, spine/leaf, Type-5 route','A <b>routed overlay</b> keeps the physical fabric as an IP network and carries tenant reachability on top. A <b>leaf</b> connects workloads or access blocks; a <b>spine</b> is transit between leaves. An <b>Ethernet Segment</b> represents a CE-facing Layer-2 attachment. EVPN <b>Type-5</b> carries an IP prefix.','Think of the underlay as roads and EVPN as a directory. The directory can be right while the road to the destination is broken.'),
('IRB, L2VNI, L3VNI, symmetric/asymmetric IRB, anycast gateway','An <b>L2VNI</b> represents a bridged Layer-2 segment. An <b>L3VNI</b> represents a tenant routing context. <b>IRB</b> combines bridging and routing. In <b>symmetric IRB</b>, ingress and egress VTEPs both route; asymmetric IRB uses a different lookup pattern and often requires broader L2VNI presence.','A host sends off-subnet traffic to its gateway; the ingress leaf routes into the tenant overlay, the far leaf receives it and performs the final delivery.'),
('Multihoming, ESI, DF, LACP, aliasing','<b>Multihoming</b> connects one downstream segment to multiple PEs. The <b>ESI</b> tells EVPN those links belong to the same Ethernet Segment. The <b>DF</b> controls designated BUM forwarding. <b>Aliasing</b> lets remote PEs use more than one advertising PE. LACP validates only the local Ethernet bundle.','Ask two separate questions: is the local bundle healthy, and does EVPN correctly understand the shared Ethernet Segment?'),
('BGP RIB, best path, Install Route, local preference, MED','The <b>BGP RIB</b> stores BGP candidate paths. <b>Best path</b> is the preferred BGP candidate. <b>Local preference</b> is an internal BGP ranking where higher is generally preferred. <b>MED</b> is a hint about preferred entry. A best BGP path still must be installed into the routing table and have a reachable next hop.','Separate peer established, route accepted into BGP, route selected in the RIB, and route actually forwardable.'),
('Prefix list, access list, route map, inbound/outbound policy, implicit action','A <b>prefix list</b> matches prefixes. A routing <b>access list</b> matches route properties. A <b>route map</b> combines matches with permit/deny or attribute changes. Inbound policy affects what you accept; outbound policy affects what you advertise; redistribution policy affects what crosses protocols.','Policy is a checkpoint in a pipeline. The same match at the entrance, middle, or exit changes a different stage of the route life cycle.'),
('Redistribution, provenance, route tag, feedback loop','<b>Redistribution</b> converts a route learned in one protocol into a new route in another. <b>Provenance</b> means remembering the original source. A route <b>tag</b> or BGP community can carry that provenance. A <b>feedback loop</b> occurs when the route returns through another boundary and is treated as new.','Imagine stamping a route “came from OSPF” before giving it to BGP; if it later comes back toward OSPF, reject the stamped copy.'),
('MAC-VRF, IP-VRF, Type-5, anycast gateway','A <b>MAC-VRF</b> is the Layer-2 EVPN tenant context. An <b>IP-VRF</b> is the Layer-3 tenant routing table. EVPN <b>Type-5</b> carries IP prefixes between routed tenant contexts.','First prove the software release supports the feature, then prove route origination, then prove VTEP forwarding.'),
('Route-map sequence, match/action, attachment point','A route map is an ordered list of rules. Each rule has an ID, match criteria and an action. FortiOS evaluates numeric rule IDs, not visual position. The attachment point decides whether the map affects received routes, advertised routes or redistribution.','Always label a route map by its pipeline stage: peer-in, redistribution, or peer-out.'),
('MP-BGP EVPN, Type-2, Type-3, ARP suppression, ingress replication','<b>MP-BGP</b> carries multiple address families. EVPN <b>Type-2</b> advertises MAC/IP endpoints. <b>Type-3</b> (IMET) describes BUM participation. <b>ARP suppression</b> answers some ARP from control-plane knowledge. <b>Ingress replication</b> makes the sender copy BUM traffic to remote VTEPs.','EVPN reduces flooding but still relies on ordinary IP routing between VTEPs.'),
('Vxlan1, source interface, VLAN-to-VNI map, head-end replication','On EOS, <b>Vxlan1</b> is the VXLAN tunnel interface. Its <b>source interface</b> supplies the VTEP address. A <b>VLAN-to-VNI map</b> ties a local Ethernet segment to an overlay segment. <b>Head-end replication</b> copies BUM traffic at the ingress VTEP.','Verify service mapping, tunnel identity and IP transport as three separate layers.'),
('EVPN DCI, re-origination, D-path, RD/RT','<b>DCI</b> means Data Center Interconnect. A DCI gateway can <b>re-originate</b> a route from one EVPN domain into another. <b>D-path</b> records domain traversal to prevent loops. RD gives uniqueness; RT controls tenant import/export.','Two EVPN domains can still behave like separate routing domains when a gateway terminates and re-originates routes.'),
('MLAG peer-link, system ID, shared/local VTEP, Type-2 vs Type-5 next hop','An <b>MLAG peer-link</b> connects the two coordinating switches. A shared system identity makes the server see one LACP system, but EVPN routes may intentionally use shared or local VTEP next hops depending on route type.','The pair can look like one switch to the server while remaining two routing nodes to BGP.'),
('FRR bgpd, zebra, Linux bridge, FDB, netlink','<b>bgpd</b> runs BGP. <b>zebra</b> coordinates routes/interfaces with the Linux kernel. A Linux <b>bridge</b> switches Layer 2. The <b>FDB</b> maps MACs to ports or VXLAN destinations. <b>netlink</b> is how zebra learns/programs kernel networking state.','On FRR, BGP correctness and Linux forwarding correctness are explicitly different layers.'),
('advertise-all-vni, underlay VRF, EVPN address family, route target','The EVPN <b>address family</b> is the MP-BGP family carrying EVPN routes. A <b>VRF</b> is an independent routing table. In FRR, <b>advertise-all-vni</b> enables EVPN-VXLAN in the selected underlay context.','Enabling the EVPN control plane does not create the route to the remote VTEP.'),
('OSPF ASBR, Type-5/Type-7 LSA, E1/E2, forwarding address, external tag','An <b>ASBR</b> injects external routes into OSPF. Normal externals use <b>Type-5 LSAs</b>; an NSSA originates <b>Type-7</b>. <b>E1</b> includes internal cost to the ASBR/forwarding address; <b>E2</b> is dominated by the external metric. The external <b>tag</b> is useful for provenance.','An LSA can exist in the LSDB while the router still cannot install a usable route from it.'),
('EVPN Type-2/Type-5 coexistence, PFE, route preference','A destination can exist as a host-specific Type-2 MAC/IP route and be covered by a Type-5 prefix. The <b>PFE</b> is Juniper forwarding hardware. Multiple route representations can consume additional next-hop resources. Junos <b>preference</b> is a local route-source ranking; lower is more preferred.','Two valid control-plane representations can become a forwarding-hardware scale issue.'),
('vrf-import/export, BGP group policy, vpn-apply-export, community','<b>vrf-import</b> controls which VPN/EVPN routes enter a routing instance; <b>vrf-export</b> controls how routes leave it. BGP group policy acts at peer scope. <b>vpn-apply-export</b> makes group export policy process VPN routes. A <b>community</b> is BGP metadata used for policy.','Decide whether the intent is tenant-wide policy or policy toward one peer; those are different scopes.'),
('Junos preference, OSPF cost, active/inactive route','<b>OSPF cost</b> ranks OSPF paths. Junos <b>preference</b> ranks route sources from different protocols. An <b>active route</b> is selected for forwarding; an inactive route remains visible but is not used.','Do not compare OSPF metric directly with IS-IS metric; first compare the protocols using local preference values.'),
('AOS-CX Active Gateway, VSX, SVI, virtual gateway','An <b>SVI</b> is a Layer-3 interface for a VLAN. <b>VSX</b> coordinates two Aruba/HPE switches. <b>Active Gateway</b> lets both present the same virtual default gateway while keeping unique physical/control-plane identities.','Hosts should see one stable gateway while the network still operates two distinct routing nodes.'),
('AOS-CX redistribution, OSPF external, route map, tag','AOS-CX can redistribute BGP, connected, static or other routes into OSPF. A <b>route map</b> decides which routes cross and can apply policy. A <b>tag</b> records origin so a second boundary can reject returning routes.','Prefix filtering answers what is allowed; tagging answers where the route came from.'),
('REST API, GET/PUT, MAC mobility, duplicate suppression','A <b>REST API</b> lets software read/change resources over HTTP. <b>GET</b> reads state. <b>PUT</b> sets/replaces a representation. <b>MAC mobility</b> means the same MAC moves between attachment points; excessive movement can trigger duplicate detection and suppress EVPN advertisement.','Automation can change EVPN control-plane behavior much faster than a human, so every API field needs a networking mental model.')]

articles=list(re.finditer(r'<article class="lesson"[^>]*>.*?</article>',s,re.S))
if len(articles)!=21: raise SystemExit(f'Expected 21 lessons, found {len(articles)}')
# Rebuild from end so offsets remain stable.
for i in range(20,-1,-1):
    m=articles[i]; block=m.group(0); terms,explain,mental=F[i]
    beginner=f'<section class="beginner"><h3>Beginner foundation — terms you need before this lesson</h3><p><b>Key terms:</b> {terms}</p><p class="plain">{explain}</p><div class="mental"><b>Beginner mental model:</b> {mental}</div><div class="why"><b>Why this matters operationally:</b> Separate what the protocol knows from what the device can actually forward. Stop at the first missing state instead of changing unrelated knobs.</div></section>'
    pos=block.find('<h3>')
    if pos<0: raise SystemExit(f'No lesson heading in lesson {i+1}')
    block=block[:pos]+beginner+block[pos:]
    s=s[:m.start()]+block+s[m.end():]

decoder='''<section class="card"><h2>Before the exam: quick acronym decoder</h2><p><b>RIB</b> Routing Information Base • <b>FIB</b> Forwarding Information Base • <b>EVPN</b> Ethernet VPN • <b>VXLAN</b> Virtual Extensible LAN • <b>VTEP</b> VXLAN Tunnel Endpoint • <b>VNI</b> VXLAN Network Identifier • <b>IRB</b> Integrated Routing and Bridging • <b>RT</b> Route Target • <b>RD</b> Route Distinguisher • <b>ESI</b> Ethernet Segment Identifier • <b>DF</b> Designated Forwarder • <b>BUM</b> Broadcast/Unknown-unicast/Multicast • <b>ASBR</b> Autonomous System Boundary Router • <b>NSSA</b> Not-So-Stubby Area • <b>LACP</b> Link Aggregation Control Protocol.</p><p>When an exam question says a route is “present,” ask where: protocol database, RIB or FIB. Those are different claims.</p></section>'''
exam=s.find('<section class="card"><h2>Cumulative 50-question exam</h2>')
if exam<0: exam=s.find('<section class="card"><h2>Cumulative exam</h2>')
if exam<0: raise SystemExit('Exam section not found')
s=s[:exam]+decoder+s[exam:]

# Validation before writing.
assert s.count('Beginner foundation — terms you need before this lesson')==21
assert 'function gradeAll' in s and 'function resetExam' in s
assert s.count('Original image URL:')==8
assert 'name="question-count" content="50"' in s or 'content="50" name="question-count"' in s
OUT.write_text(s,encoding='utf-8')
print(f'Wrote {OUT} bytes={OUT.stat().st_size}')
